  urldate = {2024-02-02},
  abstract = {Humanists offset the tradition, inherited from the ancient philosophers, of hostility between philosophy and poetry, in a number of ways. A new breed of writers, comparable to the eighteenth-century philosophes, combined literary and philosophic interests. Poets drew inspiration from the ancient philosophers, including Plato. A new genre, the "philosophical poem," sprang up. Renaissance philosophy and Humanism are not antithetical; rather, Humanism became a catalyst for both poetry and philosophy. (AOA)},
